A data protection impact assessment is required prior to processing when a type of processing, especially using new technologies, is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons, taking into account the nature, scope, context, and purposes of the processing [1]. It is particularly required for:
- a systematic and extensive evaluation of personal aspects based on automated processing, including profiling, where decisions produce legal effects or similarly significant effects [1];
- processing on a large scale of special categories of data or of personal data relating to criminal convictions and offences [1];
- systematic monitoring of a publicly accessible area on a large scale [1].

The controller must seek the advice of the data protection officer, where designated, when carrying out the assessment [1].

The documents do not cover every possible national-law or sector-specific trigger beyond these GDPR examples, but they do say the supervisory authority must make public lists of processing operations subject to, or exempt from, the requirement [3].

A risk management system for high-risk AI systems must be established, implemented, documented, and maintained [3]. It must be a continuous iterative process planned and run throughout the entire lifecycle of the system, with regular systematic review and updating [3].

It must include:
- identifying and analyzing known and reasonably foreseeable risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights when used as intended [3];
- estimating and evaluating risks under intended use and reasonably foreseeable misuse [3];
- evaluating other risks based on post-market monitoring data [3];
- adopting appropriate and targeted risk management measures to address the identified risks [3].

The measures must relate only to risks that can be reasonably mitigated or eliminated through design/development of the system or adequate technical information [3]. They must also:
- take into account the effects and interaction of the combined requirements in the Section [6];
- be designed so the residual risk for each hazard and overall residual risk is acceptable [6];
- eliminate or reduce risks as far as technically feasible through adequate design and development [6];
- where appropriate, include mitigation and control measures for risks that cannot be eliminated [6];
- provide the information required under Article 13 and, where appropriate, training to deployers [6].

When implementing the system, providers must also consider whether the high-risk AI system is likely to adversely affect persons under 18 and, as appropriate, other vulnerable groups [5]. Testing must be carried out to identify the most appropriate risk management measures, ensure consistent performance for the intended purpose, and check compliance with the relevant requirements; testing may include real-world conditions and must be done at appropriate times throughout development and in any event before market placement or service [2].

The documents do not say how a risk management system is assessed in practice beyond these required elements, or what any specific form of certification or approval process would be.
